    "reading": {
        "body": "New Mexico's 2026 legislative session produced a broad set of AI bills, but almost all of them target transparency, accountability, and chatbot safety rather than the power and buildings AI depends on.\n\nThe bill list is dominated by governance and privacy measures: the Artificial Intelligence Accountability Act, the Artificial Intelligence Transparency Act, the Artificial Intelligence Government Use Act, the Chatbot Safety Act, and the Data Broker Privacy Act. Alongside these sit a large block of health licensure compacts and data protection bills, plus the state's general appropriation acts. Only one item touches AI infrastructure directly, a Quantum Facility Infrastructure Tax Credit, and it concerns quantum rather than AI data centers. The one grid-related item in the record is not a New Mexico bill at all: a September 19 news item about Senator Heinrich offering a GRID Savings Act to make AI data centers pay for grid upgrades.\n\nThat gap matters because compute is downstream of buildings and power, and the state-level activity here is concentrated on what AI models say and how government uses them, not on whether they can be powered. A state can pass transparency and chatbot rules without resolving anything about interconnection queues, generation capacity, or who pays for substation upgrades. The Heinrich item shows the grid-cost question is live at the federal level, but nothing in New Mexico's bill list indicates the state has taken it up.\n\nWhat this data does not show is bill status, votes, or outcomes. It lists titles and dates, not whether anything passed, was amended, or died. It also does not cover utility regulation, data center siting, or power purchase agreements, so the absence of grid legislation here should be read as absence from this list, not proof that no such activity exists.",
